-81b1-b9488d698494" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152982 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152982 #edb/ref 1061 16271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152982 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3e1f0c98-c1f6-59bc-8387-51338e8475ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152982 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152983 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152983 #edb/ref 1061 16272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152983 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65b0ae62-9c2f-5d32-a1a1-ac39767edaa8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152983 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153196 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153196 #edb/ref 1061 3709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153196 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1aec00f2-ca4a-5296-be84-e7e83d0f585d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153196 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153199 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153199 #edb/ref 1061 3712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153199 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4dc61433-6c56-58c8-9a95-4614aa5f1bf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153199 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153200 #edb/ref 1061 3715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153200 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e8203c67-93c8-55d2-b5fd-7a7003ecaf79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153200 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153338 #edb/ref 1064 "How does a proportional relationship look when we plot it in the $(x,y)$ plane?\n\nTo answer this, let's consider the following proportional relationship:\n\n$$\ny = 2x\n$$\n\nTo plot a graph, we first need a table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ |  |\n| $1$ |  |\n| $2$ |  |\n| $3$ |  |\n\nFilling in the $y$-values using the equation $y = 2x$, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $3$ | $6$ |\n\nSo, we have the following $(x,y)$ coordinates:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\quad (1,2), \\quad (2,4), \\quad (3,6)\n$$\n\nIf we plot these points in the coordinate plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/a8/a8cd951c66c67b8f28b791011c7553675b3112029d9ebe4b19769127b5e75bd5.png)\n\nThis leads us to the following important fact:\n\n> *The graph of any proportional relationship is a straight line that passes through the origin $(0,0)$.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153338 #edb/ref 1065 1507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153338 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "becbb429-3b12-59c4-8172-aef250c47df9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153338 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153339 #edb/ref 1064 "When there is a proportional relationship between $x$ and $y$, we can express this using the **proportionality symbol** $\\propto$, as follows:\n$y \\propto x$\n\nNote that the following statements are all equivalent:\n\n- $y = kx$\n- $y$ **varies directly** with $x$\n- $y$ is **directly proportional** to $x$\n- $y \\propto x$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153339 #edb/ref 1065 1508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153339 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "503f0382-0b82-583d-898a-bc1dd1aa02d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153339 #edb/ref 1067 "Alternative Notations and Terminology" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
